-- TRIEL example inside the formal core: a physical system with a sensor. The
-- drive motor of an industrial robot arm overheats, and the specification
-- states what the cell must do about it.
--
-- One activation of the specification is one incident: the activation entry
-- is the moment the overheat alarm is raised, and every duration counts from
-- it (formal/core/CORE.md, section 8.3, point 5), as in
-- gdpr_art33_notification.triel. The cell controller must stop the drive
-- within 30 seconds of the alarm; a stop exactly at the deadline fulfils the
-- obligation. A missed deadline is a breach, which stays on record, and the
-- obligation is escalated: the safety engineer must then stop the drive
-- within 30 seconds of the breach (formal/core/CORE.md, section 8.3, point 10).
-- The arm must not move while the motor temperature is at or above the limit.
-- The temperature should come back below the limit within 10 minutes of the
-- alarm.
--
-- The temperature is quoted by a sensor, an ORACLE, so PROVENANCE_REQUIRED is
-- true (TECHNICAL_REPORT.md section 2.9). A reading is fresh for 5 seconds
-- after it arrives (MAX_AGE 5 SECONDS ON_STALE BLOCK); a reading that is older,
-- or present at activation without an arrival, is absent (examples/core/
-- README.md, conventions 3 and 4). So when the sensor is silent, or its data
-- are stale, the temperature is absent and:
--
-- - the condition of the prohibition is undefined. The verdict of the
--   prohibition records a breach only when its condition is true, so it stays
--   PENDING; the fail-closed monitor of the prohibition blocks the movement,
--   since the condition is not known to be false (MONITOR ... BLOCK;
--   undefined_guard_blocks_not_breach);
-- - the invariant is undefined (U): missing data neither fulfil nor violate
--   EVENTUALLY ... WITHIN (convention 11).
--
-- No reading is fresh at the activation entry, since its event is the
-- activation and not an arrival. So in this specification the invariant is
-- never F: it is T once a fresh reading below the limit arrives in time, and
-- U otherwise. F would say that the temperature was below the limit at no
-- entry up to the deadline, the alarm included, and no fresh reading shows
-- that.
--
-- Only the monitor of the prohibition stops the arm; the invariant reports,
-- it does not block.
--
-- Limit of version 2.5.1: a condition "at or above the limit for longer than
-- N seconds", counted from an arbitrary moment of the trace, is not expressible.
-- It would need a deadline inside a branch of IF or WHEN, which the translator
-- rejects (formal/core/CORE.md, section 10.6, rule 7), and the condition of IF
-- or WHEN is read once, in the first state of the trace (convention 6). The
-- example therefore counts from the activation by the alarm. Conditions
-- compare values and do not compute them: the sensor quotes the temperature,
-- and the specification does not model the motor. Every construct in this
-- file is translated into the Isabelle/HOL core (formal/core) and evaluated by
-- the exported evaluator (evaluator/).

SPECIFICATION sensor_overheat VERSION 1.0.0
    PROVENANCE_REQUIRED: true

SUBJECTS {
  controller      : SYSTEM,
  arm             : SYSTEM,
  temp_sensor     : ORACLE,
  safety_engineer : PARTY
}

TERMS {
  controller MUST stop_drive WITHIN 30 SECONDS;
  controller ON_BREACH ESCALATE_TO safety_engineer;
  arm MUST_NOT move WHEN motor_temp >= limit_c
}

FACTORS {
  motor_temp : Decimal METADATA SOURCE temp_sensor MAX_AGE 5 SECONDS ON_STALE BLOCK,
  limit_c    : Decimal METADATA
}

INVARIANTS {
  cooled_down : LIVENESS : EVENTUALLY(motor_temp < limit_c) WITHIN 10 MINUTES
}
